    {
      "title": "Comparisons and alternatives",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Adverse-event findings reported for cannabinoids versus placebo in PD include: the Fernandes et al. meta-analysis of seven randomized trials reports increased risk of confusion (risk ratio, 4.44; 95% confidence interval, 1.17-16.87) and dizziness (risk ratio, 2.78; 95% confidence interval, 1.65-4.68); the Colorado CBD/THC trial reports twice as many treatment emergent adverse events in the CBD/THC group, most of them mild; and the São Paulo cannabis-oil trial reports irritability more frequent in the CBD/THC group (P = 0.005). (medium confidence)",
          "source": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42966680/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:e6a4e7761132898c6004d5ee5ac74693cd9ddae13555d410a044c250f54bfd7b",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:2e212dc9ba275c57f165c896f8258ad0fc0882a2c9f7629459fd1fdc9c7a0fa5",
            "ev:538fafc1888b68fb621f6b724887f1ba8dfa0caa9a2a3ff3dafc34b8060d16e8",
            "ev:553edc6335cce3792d6e8d3b3ea6ca5b68461ad47c45aa78f307a27a60e8ca2d",
            "ev:5c7c5d63e4e5eda2e834180024b9b56253030b35e683d83a3c36c1976c331f6d",
            "ev:c24d7cee6a0b350ba6fa1a9aca1517edba59ccae14a4f4123fad049ecbb0241e",
            "ev:c3bc2cea2ec950b3cad85f4e63ba82fbc84c5eaaa5c3cab0434e9dccf55e679a"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11102313/",
            "https://movementdisorders.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/mds.70449"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "In Kubota et al. (Movement Disorders, first published 07 August 2026), a phase II randomized, placebo-controlled trial of an oral cannabis oil in PD patients with chronic pain (final daily dosage 43.88 mg CBD and 0.96 mg THC over 9 weeks; 101 randomized, 87 completed), the authors report no significant difference between CBD/THC and placebo in PD-PCS scores (P = 0.757) or in other non-motor scales, and the study was stopped due to futility after a pre-planned interim analysis; the authors conclude that the cannabis oil formulation did not improve pain or other non-motor symptoms compared with placebo. (low confidence)",
          "source": "https://movementdisorders.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/mds.70449",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:aad5222156bcb99fe789aabb6dfffebda4384812dfa853ec73d045f2b3cca5c9",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:582908c7b1f56dd60823caccf391441f6efeccb4eb76a9826129da482006da05",
            "ev:6e8d29a08822376a5e07327523cf4f6de3a6cf8ae511294e21035d3e885894ef",
            "ev:6e8f43b0068d238aff27a34a574e6f5eaa0145333b8ef7104e3f09e2d61e6092",
            "ev:8feffb7df6904ca253cdfcad9c891c6e66d75bd279bff5558671e60bf5ab8a23",
            "ev:9cde500c080da731a511a53917e645f6588beb8be6b52cac01c589f90a4a8624",
            "ev:ad57d9d33dcf1704eece875e6f3e6f752f72b3a70975b4acba66353f79c7ea75",
            "ev:ebafabc82a00b1805047d3ae6d54936ba0adb0f63b11ed232750e73600727a41"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Randomized trials report different results for non-motor symptoms in PD: Peball et al. (phase II enriched-enrollment randomized withdrawal trial of nabilone; 47 patients began open-label titration and 19 patients received nabilone or placebo) reported a MDS-UPDRS-I difference of 1.63 (95% CI 0.09 to 3.18, p = 0.030) at week 4 and interpret this as potential efficacy of nabilone, with one author an employee of the study-drug manufacturer; Kubota et al. (CBD/THC oil versus placebo) reported no significant differences in other non-motor scales; and the Messak et al. meta-analysis found non-motor symptoms not significantly improved (SMD= -0.03, p = 0.94). (medium confidence)",
          "source": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32757413/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:cac830c11ffec0f86b1e4aabd604ae648d16d794e208ab3fc4ef277f4708bf55",
