    {
      "title": "Facts and statements",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Systematic reviews and meta-analyses of cannabis or cannabinoids in PD, published between 2022 and 2026, conclude that the evidence does not establish clinical benefit: Urbi et al. report that no compelling evidence was found to recommend the use of cannabis in PD patients, while also identifying a potential benefit with respect to alleviation of PD related tremor, anxiety, pain, improvement of sleep quality and quality of life; Messak et al. report that cannabinoids have not shown significant benefit in PD; Fernandes et al. report that current randomized evidence is insufficient to establish clinically meaningful benefits. (high confidence)",
          "source": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34958046/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:d09c1235454843047a0c0062e5c3a02d5edc475a88bda8bfc81261a770dabf01",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:0b0c9c46d4162e24020dd424fd5d5c07d7d8f50ac61d9b0fc2a9d0eaf03d4028",
            "ev:15ca37ddc8e319f488497a144c1db85e6b72013d27437aad17298dd570dbdcf3",
            "ev:3b73ad7f3eb7876a888bb15238726375ea8a267cd67a25e389276977027677dd",
            "ev:4bc904f48a7c8280c8db690ff780c86f1f348824d9aa1cda8c8652540de48b7a",
            "ev:7a77d2b08a0ae17214b733218b1267d13e58ab89441ad75877018f9f41a0e68f",
            "ev:e86528c25f8897df901be8afa4fded489050be16732fc1a2bc61d32391a6c957",
            "ev:f49f82744ca3d1ece62555241f74a713a072cbb1d4b952926fb49e7245d2d3d7"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42138778/",
            "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42966680/"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "In the Messak et al. meta-analysis of randomized trials of cannabinoids in PD (eleven RCTs identified, six eligible for meta-analysis), cannabinoids did not significantly improve PD severity (SMD = 0.16, p = 0.27), motor examination (SMD=-0.08, p = 0.57) or non-motor symptoms (SMD= -0.03, p = 0.94); the authors state that the nabilone benefit signal for non-motor symptoms represents a distinct question warranting dedicated trials. (low confidence)",
          "source": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42138778/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:02a51870f6ab6d4247fc1bb8424eddb61324fe5b0ce6a2e61f768bfbcefe6bc8",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:87a2f327cb5b4050daca8c2e0a273bd0bc60d6954163bedcdff78cc244c6acab",
            "ev:ed2e7470524d12606897c3f680ae7294aba8cc8b82d69364fadf42f741664af4",
            "ev:f592563f42deeb55c04998862829317dc49dccfe93321c0ac97a055330a62ff1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Two small randomized crossover trials of cannabinoids for levodopa-induced dyskinesia in PD reported opposite results: Sieradzan et al. (Neurology, 2001; n = 7) reported that nabilone significantly reduced levodopa-induced dyskinesia, whereas Carroll et al. (Neurology, 2004; 19 randomized, seventeen completed) reported no evidence of a treatment effect of oral cannabis extract on levodopa-induced dyskinesia; a systematic review of RCTs in movement disorders states that of three PD RCTs with levodopa-induced dyskinesia as primary outcome, only one, using nabilone, showed a reduction. (medium confidence)",
          "source": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11739835/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:a77ad6e7adcc7307217444f4bbb8c623378f64aefcb403380acf497a46d177ee",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:0096480b22ce273f08ba3d5faabb9535a82c7fd2f2410a47fe78d4df40c3424b",
            "ev:2969fc357f19c8136f5592ff518954e621f4a323cc55d5da7c98dfdf23c8d55b",
            "ev:442c0ffa9e1dae8bcfc3ad25a73bcbf6bded00d4d84d05fb2ac8f106489caedd",
